From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 08:03:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: b43: only the NAPI poll walks the DMA RX ring

With NAPI, the hardirq handler schedules the poll when an interrupt only
signals RX and TX status, and wakes the IRQ thread otherwise. The thread
then also receives from the DMA RX ring when RX_DONE is among its reasons.
Its mask write in the hardirq handler is posted, so the level interrupt can
fire again while the thread runs; when that interrupt is a pure RX one, the
poll is scheduled and walks the same ring as the thread. Both hand the same
buffers to mac80211 and refill the slots, and one skb is freed twice.

On an R6400 v2 (BCM4331) with two stations under load, that crashed the
router twice, 22 and 23 minutes after the start: an Oops in __slab_free
on an skb head, from b43_dma_rx() in the IRQ thread. A test counter for
two walkers in the ring at once saw the overlap in 10 of 20 driver
reload cycles.

Let only the poll walk the DMA RX ring. When the thread finds RX_DONE or
a descriptor underrun, it schedules the poll and returns with the device
interrupts still masked; the poll enables them again when it is done, as
it does for the interrupts the hardirq handler gives it. The underrun
recovery moves into the poll, so it no longer races with it either. The
thread decides with b43_tx_direct(), the same test the hardirq handler
uses before it hands an interrupt to the poll, so both agree on which
devices receive in the poll (flatstik's suggestion). PIO and SDIO keep
receiving in the thread.

This changes the NAPI receive path added earlier in this series.

Tested on the R6400 v2: no overlap in 30 reload cycles with the counter;
then 2 x 75 cycles (7 h 50 min) and 200 cycles (10.5 h) of load from two
stations without a crash, stall or kernel message, at the same throughput.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Erik Conijn <egc112@msn.com>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/b43.h  |  2 ++
 drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c | 33 +++++++++++++++++++++---
 2 files changed, 32 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/b43.h
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/b43.h
@@ -954,6 +954,8 @@ struct b43_wldev {
 	struct napi_struct napi;
 	/* The interrupt the NAPI poll handles signals TX status */
 	bool napi_tx_status;
+	/* RX descriptor underrun seen by the IRQ thread, for the NAPI poll */
+	bool napi_rx_overflow;
 
 	/* encryption/decryption */
 	u16 ktp;		/* Key table pointer */
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/broadcom/b43/main.c
@@ -2105,6 +2105,7 @@ static void b43_do_interrupt_thread(stru
 	u32 reason;
 	u32 dma_reason[ARRAY_SIZE(dev->dma_reason)];
 	u32 merged_dma_reason = 0;
+	bool rx_napi;
 	int i;
 
 	if (unlikely(b43_status(dev) != B43_STAT_STARTED))
@@ -2163,18 +2164,29 @@ static void b43_do_interrupt_thread(stru
 	if (reason & B43_IRQ_NOISESAMPLE_OK)
 		handle_irq_noise(dev);
 
-	/* Check the DMA reason registers for received data. */
+	/*
+	 * Check the DMA reason registers for received data. With NAPI only
+	 * the poll walks the DMA RX ring: the hardirq handler can schedule
+	 * it while this thread runs, so receiving here too would hand the
+	 * same buffers to mac80211 twice.
+	 */
+	rx_napi = b43_tx_direct(dev);
 	if (dma_reason[0] & B43_DMAIRQ_RDESC_UFLOW) {
 		if (B43_DEBUG)
 			b43warn(dev->wl, "RX descriptor underrun\n");
-		b43_dma_handle_rx_overflow(dev->dma.rx_ring);
+		if (rx_napi)
+			WRITE_ONCE(dev->napi_rx_overflow, true);
+		else
+			b43_dma_handle_rx_overflow(dev->dma.rx_ring);
 	}
 	if (dma_reason[0] & B43_DMAIRQ_RX_DONE) {
 		if (b43_using_pio_transfers(dev))
 			b43_pio_rx(dev->pio.rx_queue);
-		else
+		else if (!rx_napi)
 			b43_dma_rx(dev->dma.rx_ring, INT_MAX, NULL);
 	}
+	rx_napi = rx_napi && (dma_reason[0] & (B43_DMAIRQ_RX_DONE |
+					       B43_DMAIRQ_RDESC_UFLOW));
 	B43_WARN_ON(dma_reason[1] & B43_DMAIRQ_RX_DONE);
 	B43_WARN_ON(dma_reason[2] & B43_DMAIRQ_RX_DONE);
 	B43_WARN_ON(dma_reason[3] & B43_DMAIRQ_RX_DONE);
@@ -2184,6 +2196,17 @@ static void b43_do_interrupt_thread(stru
 	if (reason & B43_IRQ_TX_OK)
 		b43_tx_status_irq(dev);
 
+	if (rx_napi) {
+		/*
+		 * The poll receives the frames and enables the device
+		 * interrupts again when it is done.
+		 */
+		local_bh_disable();
+		napi_schedule(&dev->napi);
+		local_bh_enable();
+		return;
+	}
+
 	/* Re-enable interrupts on the device by restoring the current interrupt mask. */
 	b43_write32(dev, B43_MMIO_GEN_IRQ_MASK, dev->irq_mask);
 
@@ -2212,6 +2235,10 @@ static int b43_napi_poll(struct napi_str
 	int done = 0;
 
 	if (likely(b43_status(dev) == B43_STAT_STARTED)) {
+		if (unlikely(READ_ONCE(dev->napi_rx_overflow))) {
+			WRITE_ONCE(dev->napi_rx_overflow, false);
+			b43_dma_handle_rx_overflow(dev->dma.rx_ring);
+		}
 		done = b43_dma_rx(dev->dma.rx_ring, budget, napi);
 		if (READ_ONCE(dev->napi_tx_status)) {
 			WRITE_ONCE(dev->napi_tx_status, false);
